//? Title: B-splines, P-splines and hierarchical spline fits
//? Subtitle: Global fits, HB/HP refinement meshes and penalties
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

> B-splines, P-splines, HB splines and HP splines

Compare four cubic spline fits to the same 241 observations: a smooth wave,
a narrow bump near x = 0.65, and deterministic oscillatory measurement errors.
Each language tab is a complete program using the native datamunge solvers
and plotting API. Run blocks from this document's directory. Python and R use
the local packages built with `just build-python` and `just build-r`; rebuild
R after the hierarchical spline bindings change.

- **B-spline:** `BSplineRegression` fits 40 global cubic basis functions by
  ordinary least squares, without a smoothing penalty. It has the same
  37 uniform knot spans on [0, 1] as the P-spline below.
- **P-spline:** `PSplineRegression` uses 40 global basis functions with a
  second-difference coefficient penalty and lambda = 0.5.
- **HB:** `HierarchicalBSpline` first fits 10 global basis functions, then adds
  a 32-coefficient local residual patch on [0.5, 0.75]. Both fits are unpenalized.
- **HP:** here this means **hierarchical penalized splines**. The same hierarchy
  calls `fit_cell_penalized` with lambda = 0.5 and difference order 2 on each patch.
  This is a use of `HierarchicalBSpline`, rather than a separate HP class.

The B-spline repeats its boundary knots; the P-spline extends uniform knots
past the boundaries. Their cubic spline spaces on [0, 1] agree: setting the
P-spline penalty to zero reproduces the B-spline predictions to numerical
precision. The positive P-spline penalty acts on its extended-basis coefficients.

For each penalized fit, the coefficients minimize

$$\|y - B\beta\|^2 + \lambda\|D_2\beta\|^2.$$

For a local patch, y in this expression is the residual after the ancestor
patches. Pass the original observations to the API; it computes that residual
itself. Child bases use local coordinates on [0, 1]. Refining the upper half of
the root and then its lower half selects [0.5, 0.75]; the intermediate cell has
no patch. All polynomial degrees stay at 3: this example's HP does not mean
hp refinement with variable polynomial degree.

The HB/HP implementation is a Forsey–Bartels additive patch hierarchy.
Independent patches do not enforce continuity at cell boundaries; this is
also different from the nested basis in `TruncatedHierarchicalBSpline`.
The table reports RMSE against the known noise-free signal at 1,001 grid
locations. Coefficient counts are stored coefficients, not effective degrees
of freedom. These illustrative settings use different spline spaces and are
not an equal-complexity model selection comparison.

The refinement figure below each fit shows the actual HB and HP cell trees.
Each horizontal bar is an instantiated cell at its refinement depth. Vertical
ticks mark the distinct knots of that cell's fitted basis, mapped from local
coordinates back to global x; repeated endpoint knots are drawn once. The
root has seven knot spans and 10 coefficients. The fitted [0.5, 0.75] patch
has 29 spans and 32 coefficients; its parent [0.5, 1] is a routing cell with
no fitted patch. Both methods use the same mesh and cubic degree: HP changes
the penalty, not the refinement geometry. These are overlapping additive
patches, so the bars do not represent disjoint leaf elements.

With these settings, the standard B-spline has the lowest signal RMSE
(0.01567). Local refinement and a fixed penalty do not automatically improve
a fit; the mesh figure explains where the hierarchical models spend their
additional coefficients.

\tabs(

\tab(C++,

```{cpp, results="markdown", nix-shell="git+file:../../../../..#cpp", cmd="c++ -std=c++23 -O2 {file} -o {bin} $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge) && {bin}", file="figures/spline_comparison.svg"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/fda/hierarchical_bspline.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

int main() {
  using dm::fda::BSplineBasis;
  using dm::fda::HierarchicalBSpline;
  const auto signal = [](double t) {
    return std::sin(2 * std::numbers::pi * t)
         + 0.8 * std::exp(-2500 * (t - 0.65) * (t - 0.65));
  };
  std::vector<double> x, y, local_y, grid, truth;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> points, local_points;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 240; ++i) {
    const double t = i / 240.0;
    const double value = signal(t) + 0.12 * std::sin(173*t) + 0.07 * std::cos(297*t);
    x.push_back(t); y.push_back(value); points.push_back({t});
    if (0.5 <= t && t <= 0.75) {
      local_points.push_back({t}); local_y.push_back(value);
    }
  }
  // Same cubic spline space; P uses extended endpoint knots and a penalty.
  const dm::BSplineRegression bs(dm::Vector<>(x), dm::Vector<>(y), 40, 3);
  const dm::PSplineRegression ps(dm::Vector<>(x), dm::Vector<>(y), 40, 0.5, 3, 2);
  const auto coarse = BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 10);
  const auto fine = BSplineBasis::open_uniform(3, 32);
  HierarchicalBSpline hb({0.0}, {1.0}), hp({0.0}, {1.0});
  hb.fit_cell(0, {coarse}, points, y);
  hp.fit_cell_penalized(0, {coarse}, points, y, {0.5}, 2);
  // h-refinement: upper half, then its lower half -> [0.5, 0.75].
  const int hb_half = hb.refine(0, 1), hp_half = hp.refine(0, 1);
  const int hb_patch = hb.refine(hb_half, 0), hp_patch = hp.refine(hp_half, 0);
  // Pass raw observations: fit_cell subtracts existing ancestor patches itself.
  hb.fit_cell(hb_patch, {fine}, local_points, local_y);
  hp.fit_cell_penalized(hp_patch, {fine}, local_points, local_y, {0.5}, 2);
  std::vector<double> hb_fit, hp_fit;
  for (int i = 0; i <= 1000; ++i) {
    const double t = i / 1000.0;
    grid.push_back(t); truth.push_back(signal(t));
    hb_fit.push_back(hb.evaluate({t})); hp_fit.push_back(hp.evaluate({t}));
  }
  const auto b_fit = bs.predict(dm::Vector<>(grid));
  const auto p_fit = ps.predict(dm::Vector<>(grid));
  const auto rmse = [&](const auto& fitted) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < grid.size(); ++i) sum += std::pow(fitted[i] - truth[i], 2);
    return std::sqrt(sum / grid.size());
  };
  std::println("| Fit | Coefficients | Signal RMSE |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |");
  std::println("| B-spline | 40 | {:.5f} |", rmse(b_fit));
  std::println("| P-spline | 40 | {:.5f} |", rmse(p_fit));
  std::println("| HB patches | 42 | {:.5f} |", rmse(hb_fit));
  std::println("| HP patches | 42 | {:.5f} |", rmse(hp_fit));
  const auto plot = dm::plot()
    .points(dm::Vector<>(x), dm::Vector<>(y), "Observations", "#8899aa88")
    .lines(dm::Vector<>(grid), dm::Vector<>(truth), "Signal", "#334155", 2.0)
    .lines(dm::Vector<>(grid), b_fit, "B-spline", "#15803d", 2.0)
    .lines(dm::Vector<>(grid), p_fit, "P-spline", "#b83280", 2.5)
    .lines(dm::Vector<>(grid), dm::Vector<>(hb_fit), "HB patches", "#d97706", 2.5)
    .lines(dm::Vector<>(grid), dm::Vector<>(hp_fit), "HP patches", "#2563eb", 3.0)
    .title("Global and hierarchical spline fits")
    .subtitle("Cubic splines; local correction on [0.5, 0.75]; lambda = 0.5")
    .x_label("x").y_label("y").size(1100, 650);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build/figs");
  // Inspect the fitted objects: cells, depths, bounds and patch knot sequences.
  auto meshes = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 1);
  const auto add_mesh = [&](const auto& model, const char* title, const char* color) {
    auto mesh = dm::plot().title(title)
      .subtitle("Bars = cells; ticks = distinct fitted knots; all degrees = 3")
      .x_label("Global x").y_label("Refinement depth")
      .xlim(0, 1).ylim(-2.45, 0.45)
      .y_ticks({0, -1, -2}, {"0", "1", "2"})
      .show_grid(false).margins(85, 35, 65, 50).size(1100, 310);
    for (int cell = 0; cell < model.cell_count(); ++cell) {
      const double lo = model.cell_min(cell)[0], hi = model.cell_max(cell)[0];
      const double level = -model.depth(cell);
      const bool fitted = model.has_spline(cell);
      mesh.segments({lo}, {level}, {hi}, {level}, fitted ? color : "#94a3b8", 5);
      if (fitted) {
        const auto& basis = model.spline(cell).bases()[0];
        auto knots = basis.knots();
        knots.erase(std::unique(knots.begin(), knots.end()), knots.end());
        for (double& t : knots) t = lo + (hi - lo) * t;
        mesh.segments(knots, std::vector<double>(knots.size(), level - .12),
          knots, std::vector<double>(knots.size(), level + .12), color, 1.5);
        mesh.text((lo + hi) / 2, level - .28,
          std::format("{} coefficients, {} spans", basis.basis_size(), knots.size() - 1),
          "#334155", 12);
      } else {
        mesh.text((lo + hi) / 2, level - .28, "Routing cell: no fitted patch", "#64748b", 12);
      }
    }
    meshes.add(mesh);
  };
  add_mesh(hb, "HB refinement mesh (unpenalized patches)", "#d97706");
  add_mesh(hp, "HP refinement mesh (penalized patches)", "#2563eb");
  meshes.size(1100, 620).save("build/figs/spline_refinement.svg");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("figures");
  std::filesystem::copy_file("build/figs/spline_refinement.svg", "figures/spline_refinement.svg",
    std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
  // Save this last so file= selects the fit comparison when the block is run.
  plot.save("build/figs/spline_comparison.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Fit | Coefficients | Signal RMSE |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| B-spline | 40 | 0.01567 |
| P-spline | 40 | 0.05015 |
